---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeroBN — "Panday Coffee Works" arrival.
 *
 * ROLE. The page's opening statement and its only h1. A FULL-BLEED photograph
 * band across the top, ruled beneath in ink, and under it the first panel of
 * the spine: rail "00 / Index" + a content cell holding a 1.35fr / .65fr grid —
 * eyebrow and the uppercase Archivo headline on the left, the lede and the two
 * joined actions on the right, both halves aligned to their BOTTOM edge.
 * Lifted from the element carrying data-component="HERO" in
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html": photo band height:56vh /
 * min-height:380px on #DEDCD6 with a 1px #17181A rule beneath, rail grid
 * minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr), body padding 44px 34px 40px, inner grid
 * minmax(0,1.35fr) minmax(0,.65fr) at 44px with align-items:end, h1
 * clamp(40px,6.2vw,92px)/.96 at -.03em with margin .14em 0 .24em and its last
 * line in #A8351B, lede 16.5px/1.72 in #3C3E42 with 24px beneath, and a button
 * row at gap:0 whose second control drops its left border so the pair shares
 * one edge.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every path is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts, so each is live in the sidebar AND inline):
 *   hero.image              — data-image-field, the full-bleed photograph
 *   hero.kicker             — the eyebrow (.kick)
 *   hero.headline           — the h1, set:html so an owner <em> takes the rust
 *   hero.headlineLines.${i} — the SAME h1, when hero.headline holds nothing but
 *                             the derived business name; see THE HEADLINE below
 *   hero.sub                — the lede
 *   hero.cta1.text + data-href-field hero.cta1.href — the ink-filled primary
 *   hero.cta2.text + data-href-field hero.cta2.href — the outlined secondary
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the rail's "00 / Index" — the design's own literal for this element and
 *     the FIRST railed label in its spine. It is NOT the section's ordinal in
 *     PageBN's render order: the header renders first and carries no rail, and
 *     TRUST takes no index either, so the spine's count and the render count
 *     are different numbers (CredentialsBN records the same rule for 07). It is
 *     the wrapper's spine label, not content: no data-field, and aria-hidden so
 *     a screen reader is not read a page number nobody wrote. It is the ONE
 *     authored string in this file and the wrapper contract is what authorises
 *     it.
 *   · the photograph's accessible name — layout.businessName and nothing else
 *   · both button hrefs, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBN
 *     actually renders
 *   · which of the two headline paths the h1 binds to
 *   · the rust tail, when the owner has not marked one (see THE SIGNATURE)
 *   · which columns exist at all, and therefore how many grid tracks there are
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("Roasted on the premises.", "Contact us", "Price list") are the DESIGNER
 *     filling in their own template. A real roastery gets an arrival that says
 *     only what that roastery said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no
 *     eyebrow over nothing, no empty action row, no 380px well-grey slab
 *     standing in for a photograph nobody uploaded, and — see THE RULES THAT
 *     ARE NOT DRAWN — no hairline under a band that is the last thing here.
 *   · NO META ROW. hero.meta1, hero.meta2 and hero.trustLine are declared by
 *     the schema and other families print them as chips under the actions.
 *     THIS design draws no such row; inventing one to use up a declared field
 *     would be a layout the designer did not draw. The fields stay editable in
 *     the sidebar and simply have no element here.
 *   · NO STAR ROW. The design has none, and a star drawn from anything other
 *     than a numeric rating field is a fabricated claim (R2). The schema's
 *     placeholder on hero.meta1 is literally '★★★★★ rated on Google'; a
 *     placeholder is not a default, lib/derive-content-defaults.ts emits no
 *     meta1 for exactly that reason, and this file prints no star glyph at all.
 *   · hero.tag / hero.photos.* — not declared by the schema. hero.photos and
 *     content.photos are READ as display fallbacks for the picture, but the
 *     HOOK is always hero.image: a data-field on a path no sidebar input owns
 *     looks editable and focuses nothing.
 *   · .reveal. This section is above the fold; it is painted, not animated in.
 *   · An aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote ("Hero"); the h1 already titles the region.
 *   · target="_blank". The design's demo markup carries it because its primary
 *     is hard-wired to a Messenger URL; here the href is the owner's and may be
 *     an in-page anchor, so navigation is left alone (the family idiom).
 *   · A caption plate (.cap). The design's hero photograph carries none.
 *   · An id on the <section>. The design gives HERO none, and the page's skip
 *     link already targets the wrapper's <main id="main">.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE, and why one heading reads two paths. This design's headline is
 * a STACK of uppercase lines whose last line turns rust — so the emphasis is a
 * COLOUR CHANGE and never an italic (Archivo has no italic worth using and the
 * design never asks for one).
 * WHICH FIELD holds the owner's headline is the catch, because two layers
 * upstream disagree:
 *   · lib/derive-content-defaults.ts auto-fills hero.headline with the BARE
 *     BUSINESS NAME on every named site (`headline: name || undefined`), and
 *     lib/astro-builder.ts's mergeShallow lets that derived value stand
 *     wherever the owner left the field unset.
 *   · lib/services/groq.service.ts — the extraction that writes the copy for a
 *     real submission — emits hero.headlineLines and NEVER hero.headline.
 * So "always prefer hero.headline" prints the business name and drops the
 * authored headline on the floor for exactly the sites that have one. The rule
 * is therefore: hero.headline wins whenever it is AUTHORED, meaning anything
 * other than the business name the derive layer fills in. When it is that
 * auto-fill (or empty), the surviving headlineLines rows win — STACKED, each
 * row its own line, because that is what this design draws.
 * Each row carries its ORIGINAL array index in data-field, so dropping a blank
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6). The rust falls on the last
 * SURVIVING row by DISPLAY position, which is R6's other half: the colour is
 * decoration and belongs to where the line is drawn, not to where it was
 * stored.
 * The field a click opens is always the field the visible text came from:
 * binding hero.headlineLines.0 to text that arrived as hero.headline (or the
 * reverse) aims an inline edit at a slot the sidebar is not showing.
 *
 * THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE. The design's h1 is two-tone because the
 * DESIGNER typed a coloured span into their own markup. No owner is going to
 * type a tag into a text box, so without a fallback the headline renders one
 * flat colour on every real site and the thing this template is recognised by
 * never ships. When the owner has NOT marked emphasis, the tail is derived from
 * THEIR OWN sentence and adds not one word — it only decides where the sentence
 * turns:
 *   • more than one line                  -> the last line
 *   • a comma, dash, colon or semicolon   -> everything after the LAST one
 *   • anything else                       -> nothing at all
 * That last case is deliberate. A single clause is a single clause, and
 * splitting inside one would lean on a word the owner never leaned on. An
 * explicit <em> always wins; this fills a silence.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). The arrival renders when it has something to SAY or SHOW:
 * a headline, a lede, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT count the
 * eyebrow (derive-content-defaults builds one from city + trade for sites with
 * neither name, tagline nor upload) and does NOT count the two buttons (derive
 * sets cta1/cta2 text UNCONDITIONALLY, so counting either makes the gate a
 * no-op that always passes).
 * FOR REVIEW: this is the one section in the family whose gate counts a
 * headline, which R5 otherwise forbids. Here the headline IS the body — it is
 * the document's only h1, and a hero dropped for having "only" an h1 takes the
 * page's heading with it and opens the site on the trust row. The gate is still
 * not a no-op: with no name, no tagline, no descriptor and no upload, derive
 * emits no headline, no headlineLines and no sub, and the section does not
 * render.
 *
 * THE RULES THAT ARE NOT DRAWN. Three bottom hairlines could stack here and the
 * design draws exactly one at each place:
 *   · the inner rail panel carries .sec for its tracks but NOT .sec's bottom
 *     rule — the section's own .sec-full rule is the one the design draws, and
 *     leaving both would print 2px where the design prints 1px.
 *   · the photograph's rule exists only because something follows it. With no
 *     headline, no lede and no actions the panel below is not rendered at all,
 *     and the band drops its rule rather than hairlining a row that is not
 *     there.
 *   · a column that has nothing in it is not an empty cell — it is one track
 *     fewer, so the surviving half takes the full width instead of sitting
 *     beside a hole.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH ⇒ NO BAND. The picture is a full-bleed band, not a background.
 * Rendering it with no upload leaves a 380px slab of --well above the fold; the
 * band is dropped whole and the panel becomes the top of the page.
 *
 * THE JOINED EDGE. The design's two actions sit at gap:0 with the second one's
 * left border removed, so they read as one control split in two. That is done
 * with an ADJACENT-SIBLING rule rather than a class on the second button, so a
 * secondary that renders alone (owner cleared the primary's label) keeps all
 * four of its borders instead of hanging open on the left.
 *
 * COLOUR (R1 — no literal hex in this file, only PageBN's tokens):
 *   photo band --well · its rule and the section's --rule-strong · eyebrow and
 *   headline tail --accent · headline --ink (from the wrapper's h1 rule) ·
 *   lede --ink-2 · primary .btn--ink (--ink on --on-ink) · secondary --ink on
 *   --paper inside a 1px --ink border, inverting to --ink/--on-ink on hover.
 *   --ink-2 IS the design's lede colour (#3C3E42) and R1 says map the hex to
 *   the token holding that value. Note the consequence, since it is a choice
 *   and not an oversight: buildOverrideCss repaints --paper but not --ink-2, so
 *   a scheme that darkens the ground moves the lede's background without moving
 *   the lede. --ink-soft would track the ground but is a different colour from
 *   the one the design specifies.
 *
 * CONTRAST, computed here over PageBN's own hex values by the WCAG 2.x sRGB
 * relative-luminance formula rather than copied from anywhere:
 *   --ink on --paper          15.30   (headline, secondary label)
 *   --ink-2 on --paper         9.23   (lede)
 *   --accent on --paper        5.67   (eyebrow at 10px, headline tail)
 *   --on-ink on --ink         15.30   (primary label, and the secondary's
 *                                      hover state)
 *   --accent on --well         4.80   (nothing here uses it; listed because the
 *                                      photo band is the one --well ground)
 * These agree with PageBN's own VERIFIED CONTRAST table, which states 15.30 for
 * ink/paper, 5.67 for accent/paper and 5.77 for ink-soft/paper. Every pair above
 * clears AA for its size; the tightest one drawn here is the 10px rust eyebrow,
 * at 5.67 against AA's 4.5.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .pd-arrival,
 * NOT .hero — lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts force-paints `.hero .btn-ghost` and
 * `.hero .btn-primary:hover` with !important and would strand a label here.
 * Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow / .btn-light / .nav-cta /
 * .nav-right / .trust / .testi / .cta-band in this family. The `pd-` prefix is
 * THIS template's (Panday) and is deliberately not the BM sibling's `tc-` nor
 * the BL sibling's `kc-`: an audit had to rename a whole set of classes that
 * were copied verbatim from the template before them.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;


// Anchor guard, copied from the BM sibling and re-pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes '#visit' for hero.cta1 and
// '#services' for hero.cta2; PageBN renders `visit` but not `services`, so one
// is kept and the other is remapped. Resolve every in-page href against what
// PageBN actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this design
// carries the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the target
// the design was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http) pass
// through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBN — and with the copies
// in HeaderBN, CtaBandBN and FooterBN. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the
// copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  location: 'visit', contact: 'visit',
  services: 'menu', rooms: 'menu',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};

// Display fallbacks only — the HOOK below is always hero.image, the path the
// sidebar's image picker writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an
// apostrophe from closing the url('') early and killing the declaration.
const heroImage = String(hero.image || (hero.photos && hero.photos[0]) || (content.photos && content.photos[0]) || '');
const heroImageCss = heroImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const kicker = hero.kicker || '';

// THE HEADLINE — full reasoning in the doc comment. hero.headline is this
// design's field; the rows take over only when hero.headline is nothing but the
// business name derive-content-defaults auto-filled (or is empty), which is the
// shape every AI-written submission arrives in.
const bizName = String(layout?.businessName ?? '').trim();
const headline = String(hero.headline ?? '').trim();
const lineRows: Array<{ text: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(hero.headlineLines) ? hero.headlineLines : [])
  .map((t: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(t ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { text: string }) => l.text.trim() !== '');
// …and only when they SAY something hero.headline does not. On the ordinary
// named site both hold the same one line, and the single-line hook keeps it.
const useRows =
  lineRows.length > 0 &&
  (headline === '' || headline === bizName) &&
  lineRows.map((l) => l.text.trim()).join(' ') !== headline;
const hasHeadline = useRows || headline !== '';

// ── THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// The lines are JOINED WITH <br> rather than a space: this design's h1 is a
// stack, not a flowing sentence, and an owner who typed two lines meant two.
const hasOwnEm = (v: string): boolean => /<em[\s>]/i.test(v);
const emphasise = (raw: string): string => {
  if (!raw || hasOwnEm(raw)) return raw;
  const lines = raw.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (lines.length > 1) {
    const tail = lines.pop() as string;
    return lines.join('<br>') + '<br><em>' + tail + '</em>';
  }
  // The LAST break wins, so a sentence with two commas emphasises its close
  // rather than its middle. The lookahead keeps a trailing comma from matching.
  const re = /[,;:—–]\s+(?=\S)/g;
  let cut = -1;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null) cut = m.index + m[0].length;
  if (cut <= 0 || cut >= raw.length) return raw;
  return raw.slice(0, cut) + '<em>' + raw.slice(cut) + '</em>';
};
const headlineHtml = emphasise(headline);
// The rows path marks its LAST SURVIVING row instead, and only when no row is
// already carrying the owner's own <em>.
const rowsOwnEm = lineRows.some((l) => hasOwnEm(l.text));

const sub = hero.sub || '';
const cta1Text = hero.cta1?.text || '';
// The design's primary is its contact action and its secondary points at the
// price list; those are the two fallbacks when an owner's href resolves to
// nothing this page draws.
const cta1Href = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#visit');
const cta2Text = hero.cta2?.text || '';
const cta2Href = onPage(hero.cta2?.href, '#menu');

// The photograph's accessible name is the owner's own words or nothing. With no
// business name there is no honest name for the picture, so it leaves the
// accessibility tree instead of announcing a phrase I invented. Built as ONE
// spread object so a conditional attribute can never end up beside a literal
// (R9 — two components in an earlier family shipped exactly that duplication,
// and the literal aria-hidden won).
const photoA11y: Record<string, string> = bizName
  ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': bizName }
  : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };

// The two halves of the panel, and the panel itself. Each disappears rather
// than rendering as an empty cell under a rule.
const hasSay = !!(kicker || hasHeadline);
const hasAct = !!(sub || cta1Text || cta2Text);
const hasPanel = hasSay || hasAct;
const hasArrival = !!(hasHeadline || sub || heroImage);
---
{hasArrival && (
<section class:list={['sec-full', 'pd-arrival', !hasPanel && 'pd-arrival--bare']}>
  {/* The full-bleed band. No upload ⇒ no band at all, rather than 380px of
      --well standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
  {heroImage && (
    <div class="plate pd-ar-band">
      <div
        class="ph pd-ar-ph"
        data-image-field="hero.image"
        style={`background-image:url('${heroImageCss}');`}
        {...photoA11y}
      ></div>
    </div>
  )}

  {hasPanel && (
    <div class="sec pd-ar-panel">
      <div class="rail">
        {/* DERIVED and decorative: the spine's index for section 00. Never a
            hook, never stored, and hidden from the accessibility tree. */}
        <span class="rail-t" aria-hidden="true">Index</span>
      </div>
      <div class="sec-body">
        <div class:list={['pd-ar-grid', !(hasSay && hasAct) && 'pd-ar-grid--one']}>
          {hasSay && (
            <div class="pd-ar-say">
              {kicker && <span class="kick pd-ar-kick" data-field="hero.kicker">{kicker}</span>}
              {!useRows && headline !== '' && <h1 class="pd-ar-h1" data-field="hero.headline" set:html={headlineHtml}></h1>}
              {useRows && (
                <h1 class="pd-ar-h1">
                  {lineRows.map((l, k) => (
                    <span
                      class:list={['pd-ar-ln', !rowsOwnEm && k === lineRows.length - 1 && 'pd-ar-ln--em']}
                      data-field={`hero.headlineLines.${l.i}`}
                      set:html={l.text}
                    ></span>
                  ))}
                </h1>
              )}
            </div>
          )}

          {hasAct && (
            <div class="pd-ar-act">
              {sub && <p class="lede pd-ar-lede" data-field="hero.sub">{sub}</p>}
              {(cta1Text || cta2Text) && (
                <div class="pd-ar-acts">
                  {cta1Text && (
                    <a class="btn btn--ink pd-ar-b1" href={cta1Href} data-field="hero.cta1.text" data-href-field="hero.cta1.href">{cta1Text}</a>
                  )}
                  {cta2Text && (
                    <a class="btn pd-ar-b2" href={cta2Href} data-field="hero.cta2.text" data-href-field="hero.cta2.href">{cta2Text}</a>
                  )}
                </div>
              )}
            </div>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  )}
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBN's token vocabulary
     (--paper --well --ink --ink-2 --on-ink --accent --rule-strong --mono --r)
     and from its shared primitives (.sec .sec-full .rail .rail-t .sec-body
     .kick .lede .btn .btn--ink .plate .ph). There is no literal hex in this
     file, and no radius either: this design's --r is 0 and .plate already
     carries it.

     HOW THESE RULES WIN. Astro scopes a style block with the project's
     `scopedStyleStrategy`, and astro.config.mjs sets none, so it takes the
     default 'attribute' — `.pd-ar-band` ships as
     `.pd-ar-band[data-astro-cid-…]`, specificity (0,2,0). The wrapper's
     primitives are `html[data-page="BN"] :where(.plate)`, specificity (0,1,1),
     because :where() contributes nothing. Two classes beat one class plus one
     element, which is how the band's height replaces .plate's 180px floor, the
     panel drops .sec's bottom rule, the h1 keeps its .14em margins against the
     shared `margin:0`, and the lede takes the design's colour — none of it with
     !important.
     Setting scopedStyleStrategy:'where' in astro.config.mjs would collapse
     every one of those to (0,1,0) and silently hand the wrapper the win. Two-
     class rules survive that; the single-class ones do not. */

  /* ── THE PHOTOGRAPH BAND. Design: height:56vh; min-height:380px;
     background:#DEDCD6 (which is --well, already on .plate); border-bottom:1px
     solid #17181A (which is --rule-strong). .plate reserves a 180px minimum for
     short plates; this band states its own, so that floor is released. */
  .pd-ar-band {
    height: 56vh;
    min-height: 380px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
  }
  /* The rule under the band exists because a panel follows it. When there is no
     panel the band is the last thing in the section, and the section's own
     .sec-full rule is already drawing that line — two would print 2px. */
  .pd-arrival--bare .pd-ar-band { border-bottom: 0; }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class would, so `.pd-ar-ph` alone would
     lose to it. Restating cover/center costs nothing and means the photograph
     cannot letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. No colour is restated —
     that stays in the primitive, where the theme engine keeps control of it. */
  .pd-ar-band .pd-ar-ph {
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* ── THE PANEL. .sec supplies the 52px rail track and the content cell; only
     its bottom rule is dropped, because the <section> carries .sec-full and the
     design draws ONE line at the foot of the arrival, not two. */
  .pd-ar-panel { border-bottom: 0; }

  /* ── THE INNER GRID. Design: grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.35fr)
     minmax(0,.65fr); gap:44px; align-items:end. The bottom alignment is the
     point: the headline's baseline and the button row settle on the same line,
     which is what makes the panel read as a ruled document rather than two
     stacked blocks. */
  .pd-ar-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.35fr) minmax(0, .65fr);
    gap: 44px;
    align-items: end;
  }
  /* One half missing ⇒ one track. The survivor takes the width instead of
     sitting beside a 44px gap and an empty cell. */
  .pd-ar-grid--one { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  /* min-width:0 lets a long word wrap inside its track instead of widening it
     and pushing the page sideways. */
  .pd-ar-say,
  .pd-ar-act { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE EYEBROW IS NOT RESTATED. Its 10px mono, its .24em tracking and its rust
     live on the shared .kick. Only a wrap safety is added, and in two places:
     max-width here, overflow-wrap at 520. */
  .pd-ar-kick { max-width: 100%; }

  /* Design: font-size:clamp(40px,6.2vw,92px); line-height:.96;
     margin:.14em 0 .24em. Larger and one notch tighter than the shared .h2,
     which is why this is an h1 rule of its own and not that primitive. The
     weight (800), the -.03em tracking, the uppercase and the --ink all come
     from the wrapper's own h1 rule and are deliberately not restated. The
     design sets NO max-width — its column is the measure — so none is invented
     here; the narrow query below adds one only once the columns collapse. */
  .pd-ar-h1 {
    font-size: clamp(40px, 6.2vw, 92px);
    line-height: .96;
    margin: .14em 0 .24em;
  }
  /* THE SIGNATURE is a COLOUR CHANGE, not an italic — font-style is pinned
     normal so a browser's default <em> italic cannot creep into a face that has
     no italic worth using. :global() because Astro cannot scope markup that
     arrived through set:html (R3), which covers both the owner's own <em> and
     the derived tail on either path. */
  .pd-ar-h1 :global(em) { font-style: normal; color: var(--accent); }
  /* Rows path only (hero.headlineLines). They STACK — one line each — because
     that is what this design draws. */
  .pd-ar-ln { display: block; }
  .pd-ar-ln--em { color: var(--accent); }

  /* Design: font-size:16.5px; line-height:1.72; color:#3C3E42; margin:0 0 24px.
     The size is the shared .lede verbatim (its clamp tops out at exactly
     16.5px); the line-height, the colour and the 24px are this design's and are
     stated here. max-width is released because the .65fr column is the measure
     the designer drew, not a 52ch text block. */
  .pd-ar-lede {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.72;
    max-width: none;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
  }

  /* Design: display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:0 — the two controls SHARE AN
     EDGE. */
  .pd-ar-acts {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0;
  }
  /* The outlined secondary. PageBN ships four button fills and no outline, and
     this is the one control in the design that is drawn as a hairline box:
     transparent ground, 1px --ink, --ink label, 22px of side padding instead of
     .btn's 24px. Nothing here is a new colour — it is --ink on --paper. */
  .pd-ar-b2 {
    background-color: transparent;
    color: var(--ink);
    border-color: var(--ink);
    padding-left: 22px;
    padding-right: 22px;
    /* .btn EASES background-color, which is right for a fill that only deepens
       and wrong for the inverting hover below: the label colour swaps at once
       while the ground is still most of the way to --paper. --on-ink and
       --paper are the SAME hex, so such a fade OPENS at 1:1 — the label is its
       own background — and only reaches this pair's 15.30 once the ground has
       finished travelling to --ink. The blends in between are not palette
       colours and carry no verified ratio, but the opening frames are plainly
       unreadable. Dropping background-color from the animated set makes ground
       and label change on the same frame. box-shadow stays so the wrapper's
       focus ring keeps its fade, and border-color is kept because .btn's
       duration and timing lists are positional. */
    transition-property: box-shadow, border-color;
  }
  /* The design gives its outlined control no hover; a control with none reads
     as dead. Inverting to the filled state is the smallest honest affordance
     and reuses the pair already on .btn--ink — 15.30:1, and now reached in one
     step rather than through a fade that opens with the label sitting on its
     own colour. */
  .pd-ar-b2:hover {
    background-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--on-ink);
    border-color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* THE JOINED EDGE, done as an adjacent sibling rather than a class on the
     button: a secondary rendering ALONE (owner cleared the primary's label)
     must keep all four borders instead of hanging open on the left. */
  .pd-ar-b1 + .pd-ar-b2 { border-left: 0; }

  /* ── R8 · this section's OWN grid. The wrapper owns the shared 760 pass (the
     rail goes horizontal) and the shared 520 pass (.kick and .btn shrink); the
     two content columns are this file's problem and collapse here.

     THE CLAMP LADDER, traced across every breakpoint because two audits found
     type that GREW as the viewport narrowed:
       h1  base clamp(40px,6.2vw,92px): 92 at 1484px+, 55.8 at 900, and already
           at its 40px FLOOR from 645px down. Nothing to re-scale at 900, so no
           rule is written there — a cap written at 900 would have to sit above
           55.8px to avoid a jump anyway. The 520 query then caps at exactly
           40px, which is what the base hands over at that width, and descends
           from there: 92 → 55.8 → 40 → 40 → 32.4 at 360 → 30 floor. Monotonic.
       band 56vh/380px min above 520, 44vh/240px min below it. Both terms fall,
           so the photograph can never get taller on a smaller screen.
       b2   22px of side padding above 520, 18px below — which is also the
           wrapper's own 520 padding, so the pair does not end up mismatched
           (the primary drops to 18px there and this rule would otherwise hold
           the secondary at 22px). */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pd-ar-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 32px;
      align-items: start;
    }
    /* Full width now, so the headline takes a measure instead of a column.
       15ch is the measure the design's own left column gives it at its drawn
       size, not a number invented for the phone. */
    .pd-ar-h1 { max-width: 15ch; }
    .pd-ar-lede { margin-bottom: 20px; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
       anywhere, and a long uppercase label wraps inside a full-width button
       instead of widening the row. */
    .pd-ar-band { height: 44vh; min-height: 240px; }
    .pd-ar-grid { gap: 26px; }
    .pd-ar-kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .pd-ar-h1 { font-size: clamp(30px, 9vw, 40px); max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .pd-ar-lede { overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    /* white-space:normal lets a label wrap at its spaces; overflow-wrap covers
       the label that HAS none. Without the second declaration one long unbroken
       word runs straight out of the button box at 360px — the wrapper's
       overflow-x:hidden suppresses the scrollbar and the label is clipped
       instead, which is the same bug wearing a different coat. */
    .pd-ar-acts .btn { flex: 1 1 100%; white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; text-align: center; }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's 520 button padding — see the ladder note above. */
    .pd-ar-b2 { padding-left: 18px; padding-right: 18px; }
    /* Stacked, the pair no longer shares a VERTICAL edge, so the left border
       comes back and the top one goes instead: two 1px --ink borders meeting
